    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2026-1304",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Regional climate anomalies have historically driven price increases that are followed by social conflict and economic decline. Integrated Assessment Model (IAM) studies of future climate damages to the global economy focus on the real (inflation\u2013adjusted) GDP Y yet provide no direct guidance on the inflationary response. Here, we introduce a highly aggregated framework for the world economy in which inflation naturally emerges as an intrinsic rather than exogeneous property of the macroeconomic system. A quantity \"Wealth\" is introduced defined as a single time-varying stock of historically cumulative, inflation\u2011adjusted output W (t) = \u222b0t Y(u) du. Wealth grows with nominal output YN = \u03b2W and shrinks with decay at rate \u03b3W, so that real output becomes Y = (\u03b2 \u2212 \u03b3) W. In this case, the inflation rate becomes i = \ud835\ude93 / (1 \u2212 J) where J = \u03b3 / \u03b2. Climate damages to Y represented by standard IAM quadratic damage functions are shown to scale with \u03b3. But, even for high-end damages and global mean temperature increases, the inflation increment is small. However, global inflationary shocks cannot be ruled out. Wealth W has been tethered through a scaling factor to the world\u2019s rate of primary energy consumption E. If climate change and other stresses redirect nominal output from civilization expansion to maintenance, and primary energy consumption relaxes from its current expansion rate of ~2 % per year towards stagnation, then J \u2192 1. In this limit, stagflation and even hyperinflation emerge as key economic signatures of climate change, in line with historical evidence, and plausibly emerging this century.",
    "field": "Climate change economics",
    "problem": "Existing Integrated Assessment Models (IAMs) focus on real (inflation-adjusted) economic damages from climate change but lack a theoretical framework for estimating the inflationary response, treating it as an external rather than an intrinsic economic property.",
    "method": "The paper proposes a macroeconomic framework defining \"Wealth\" as cumulative real output, where inflation emerges as an intrinsic property linked to an economic decay rate, allowing for the estimation of price increases caused by climate damages that accelerate this decay.",
    "task": "The task is to develop a macroeconomic framework to assess the impact of climate change on global economic inflation by treating inflation as an intrinsic, rather than exogenous, property of the world economy.",
